C++ supports both compile-time and runtime polymorphism.  Compile-time polymorphism, achieved through function overloading and templates, resolves function calls at compile time.  Runtime polymorphism, achieved through virtual functions and inheritance, resolves function calls at runtime based on the object's dynamic type. Understanding the difference is crucial for writing efficient and correct code.  Choosing the wrong type of polymorphism can lead to unexpected behavior or performance issues.


**Example:**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
class Shape {
public:
    virtual double area() { return 0.0; }
};
class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    Circle(double r) : radius(r) {}
    double area() override { return 3.14159 * radius * radius; }
private:
    double radius;
};
class Square : public Shape {
public:
    Square(double s) : side(s) {}
    double area() override { return side * side; }
private:
    double side;
};
int main() {
    std::vector<Shape*> shapes;
shapes.push_back(new Circle(5));
shapes.push_back(new Square(4));
    double totalArea = 0.0;
    for (Shape* shape : shapes) {
        totalArea += shape->area(); // Runtime polymorphism in action
    }
    std::cout << "Total area: " << totalArea << std::endl;
    //Remember to delete dynamically allocated objects to prevent memory leaks!
    for (Shape* shape : shapes) {
        delete shape;
    }
    return 0;
}

```

This focuses on `std::numeric_limits`, a class template providing compile-time information about arithmetic types.  Its implementation reveals fascinating aspects of compiler behavior and interaction with the underlying hardware.  The compiler doesn't just provide values; it uses these values during compilation to perform optimizations and enforce type-safe constraints. Understanding its behavior requires delving into how compilers generate code based on type properties known only at compile time. This impacts code generation, particularly in template metaprogramming and constant expression evaluation.


**Syntax:**
```cpp
#include <limits>

template <typename T>
constexpr bool isWithinRange(T value) {
  return value >= std::numeric_limits<T>::lowest() && value <= std::numeric_limits<T>::max();
}

int main() {
  constexpr bool withinRange = isWithinRange(100); // Compile-time evaluation
  static_assert(withinRange, "Value out of range"); // Compile-time check
  return 0;
}
```


**Example:**
```cpp
