In C++, std::auto_ptr was a smart pointer introduced to manage dynamic object lifetime with ownership semantics. It ensures that only one auto_ptr owns a given object at a time, transferring ownership during assignment or copy operations. This transfer of ownership is explicit and prevents multiple pointers from owning the same resource, thus avoiding double deletions. However, auto_ptr's ownership transfer semantics can lead to unexpected null pointers after assignment, making it unsafe and deprecated in modern C++ standards in favor of std::unique_ptr. Understanding auto_ptr's transfer semantics is crucial for grasping resource management and ownership transfer in C++.


**Syntax:**
template<typename T>
class auto_ptr {
public:
    explicit auto_ptr(T* p = nullptr); // Constructor
    auto_ptr(const auto_ptr& other); // Copy constructor (transfers ownership)
    auto_ptr& operator=(auto_ptr& other); // Assignment operator (transfers ownership)
    ~auto_ptr(); // Destructor
    T* release(); // Releases ownership
    T* get() const; // Access raw pointer
    void reset(T* p = nullptr); // Replace managed object
private:
    T* ptr;
};

