*   **`std::declval<T>()`**: This utility function returns an rvalue reference to an object of type `T` without requiring `T` to be default-constructible or even constructible at all. It's purely for use within `decltype` expressions.
*   **`decltype(...)`**: This operator inspects the type of an expression. In this context, `decltype(std::declval<Fn>()(std::declval<Args>()...))` asks the compiler: "If I were to call an object of type `Fn` with arguments of types `Args...`, what would be the return type of that expression?"

