In C++, namespaces organize code into logical groups, and friend declarations specify non-member functions or classes that can access the private and protected members of a class. The rules governing how names declared as friends are looked up depend on whether they are declared within a namespace or as friends, and whether they are qualified or unqualified. Understanding these lookup rules is essential for correctly designing and accessing friends in complex namespace hierarchies, ensuring proper encapsulation and access control in modern C++ programs.


**Syntax:**
In a namespace:

namespace N {
    class A {
        friend void f(); // unqualified friend declaration
        friend class B; // friend class declaration
    };
}

Friend declaration inside class:

class C {
    friend void g(); // unqualified friend declaration
    friend class D; // friend class
};

Note: Friend functions and classes are not members of the class or namespace; they are granted access but do not affect lookup outside their declaration context.

