On a 64-bit system, a `std::vector<int>` object itself is typically 24 bytes. It internally manages three pointers (or equivalent `size_t` members) to track its allocated memory on the heap:
1.  **`_M_start`**: Pointer to the beginning of the allocated array (e.g., `int*`).
2.  **`_M_finish`**: Pointer to one past the last actual element (e.g., `int*`).
3.  **`_M_end_of_storage`**: Pointer to one past the end of the allocated capacity (e.g., `int*`).

Since `std::stack` is a container adapter and has no additional data members beyond its underlying container, a `std::stack<int, std::vector<int>>` object `myStack` will occupy 24 bytes on the stack frame of `demonstrate_stack_operations`.

