In modern C++, smart pointers such as std::unique_ptr are used to manage dynamic memory with automatic resource management, ensuring exception safety and preventing memory leaks. Unlike raw pointers, std::unique_ptr enforces strict ownership semantics, meaning only one unique_ptr can own a given resource at a time. When the unique_ptr goes out of scope, it automatically deletes the managed object, following the RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) principle. This concept is essential for writing safe, efficient, and modern C++ code that manages resources without manual delete calls.


**Syntax:**
std::unique_ptr<Type> ptr = std::make_unique<Type>(constructor_args);

